Hello team,

As per we are CTPAT certified, please see below regarding the Executive =
order 14411.=20

Executive Order 14411, signed on June 3, 2026, will fundamentally change =
the responsibilities of CTPAT-validated customs brokers (CVCB) by =
mandating a higher standard of due diligence when they represent foreign =
Importers of Record (IORs). Under Section 2(c)(i) of the order, foreign =
IORs will be required to be validated in the CTPAT program; if they are =
not, they will be required to utilize a licensed CVCB to file entries =
with CBP. This CVCB requirement will ensure that foreign entities are =
held to the same rigorous standards as U.S.-based importers, which will =
help protect national security and the domestic economy from systemic =
inefficiencies and bad actors.  To implement this requirement, CBP will =
take steps to revise importer eligibility regulations, guidance, and =
policies.

To comply with these upcoming heightened expectations, in the near =
future, CVCB will need to perform comprehensive vetting of their foreign =
clients before they conduct any customs business. CBP foresees that =
during vetting, CVCB will verify critical information, such as the =
IOR=E2=80=99s legal identity, ownership structure, business =
affiliations, U.S. assets, and their history of compliance and import =
activity. Furthermore, brokers will need to assess the IOR=E2=80=99s =
ability to pay duties, taxes, and fees, and verify details regarding the =
supply chain, product classification, valuation, and country of origin.  =
CVCB will be expected to maintain thorough records of this vetting =
process powers of attorney, and all relevant communications, to =
demonstrate that they have exercised appropriate due diligence.

The order also directs that all IORs will be required to maintain "good =
standing" with CBP, as outlined in Section 2(d). Consistent with future =
regulatory requirements, IORs who lose their good standing with CBP, may =
be prohibited from importing goods or designating a broker to act on =
their behalf. To further reinforce compliance with U.S. customs and =
trade laws, Section 4(a) directs the imposition of significant penalties =
on brokers who fail to conduct required due diligence, repeatedly =
represent non-compliant or unverifiable clients, or fail to cooperate =
with CBP requests for information. These consequences will include =
financial penalties, increased audit frequency, and the potential =
suspension or removal of the CVCB from the CTPAT program. Additionally, =
Section 4(c) establishes a minimum penalty floor of 50 percent of the =
assessed penalty for violations, while eliminating mitigation options =
for repeat offenders, which will further underscore the commitment to =
strict customs enforcement.

The new requirements under the order will create a distinct competitive =
advantage for CVCB. By positioning themselves as the preferred partners =
for foreign IORs as the order is implemented by CBP, these brokers will =
be able to market their services as a necessary solution for certain =
clients. Because foreign IORs will be required to use a CVCB to file =
entries if they are not validated themselves, brokers who have already =
achieved this status will become the primary, and perhaps only, viable =
option for an increased market share of U.S imports. CTPAT validation =
will serve as a powerful badge of trust and reliability for brokers in =
an increasingly more secure trade environment.


Please note the foreign and domestics importers should contact us =
directly for the requirements and also for the Power of Attorney.

